Here's the version as a module, so you can check many strings in a loop without reading from the keyboard each time. The `if` block at the end only runs when the file is run directly, so it doesn't interfere when the module is loaded.

```perl
package Palindrome;
use strict;
use warnings;
use Exporter 'import';

our @EXPORT_OK = qw(is_palindrome);

sub normalise {
    my ($s) = @_;
    $s =~ s/[^\p{Word}++]//g;
    return lc($s);
}

sub is_palindrome {
    my ($text) = @_;
    die "is_palindrome needs a string, got ", ref($text // 'an undef'), "\n"
      unless defined $text;
    my $clean = normalise($text);
    my $reversed = join '', reverse split //, $clean;
    return $clean eq $reversed;
}

1;
```

