A few things are worth knowing. The check is case-insensitive because the string gets lowercased before it is compared with its reverse. So "Racecar" and "RACECAR" both come out as palindromes, and so does "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama". If you want upper and lower case to count as different, take out the `lc` in `normalise`.

The script only trims the outside. Spaces and punctuation inside the string stay, and so do the spaces between the words, so "not a palindrome" doesn't become "notapalindrome": it stays as it came in, and the check runs on the letters inside it.

One thing that trips up most implementations, including this one, is the odd length. In an odd-length string the middle character is its own mirror, and it always matches itself. So "radar", "level" and "a" are all palindromes, and so is "red rum, sir, as you are", which is a line from a story by Isaac Asimov.

