The program below reads from standard input, so you can type text at the prompt, or send it in with a pipe. The string comes first, typed entirely, and the program only starts reading the pattern once it has seen a line that contains no backslash. That lets you type a pattern such as `a\\b` and have it passed to the compiler as `a\b`, which is the usual trouble spot. Once the pattern is complete the program compiles it, counts the matching lines, and prints the result.

If the pattern is no good, it prints the error and leaves with a non-zero code. If the string is empty, it says so. If no pattern was given, it asks for one.

The `hasNextLine()` check is there for when the input ends before the pattern is finished, for instance when you pipe in three lines and forget the fourth. Without it the program would hang waiting for a line that was never coming. If you would rather treat that case as an empty pattern, change it to `pattern = ""; break;`.

The flag is set once, when the first line of the string is reached, and never cleared. So the search begins on that line and goes on to the end, and any line before it, which there should be none of, is left out.

The counts come with the line itself, in the same order as the input, so you can scroll back and see what matched. The column of counts sits to the right, and the program computes its width from the total, so the numbers always line up even when one line matches several times.

The pattern is shown as you typed it, inside slashes. The compile error, which gives the position inside the pattern and the reason, goes on its own line underneath.

One thing is not fixed by the program, and you should know it before you trust the numbers. The count for a line is the number of matches on that line. A line that matches the pattern is counted once, whatever way you choose to count it.

